1
00:00:00,932 --> 00:00:10,615
Welcome to Europe episode three of six. First, I clear the chosen names so we
can study a fresh regional group.

2
00:00:11,275 --> 00:00:27,993
Eight places connect the central crossroads and the Alps: Germany,
Switzerland, Liechtenstein, Austria, Poland, Czechia, Slovakia and Hungary.
Liechtenstein uses a labelled supplemental map.

3
00:00:28,653 --> 00:00:32,578
Let's bring Germany and its neighbours into a closer view.

4
00:00:35,525 --> 00:00:46,438
Germany is central Europe’s broad northern anchor, south of Denmark and east
of the Netherlands, Belgium and France. Poland and Czechia lie along its
eastern side.

5
00:00:47,096 --> 00:01:00,482
German reunification took effect on October third, 1990. The Berlin Wall had
fallen the previous year, making the two dates related but different.

6
00:01:01,142 --> 00:01:15,306
Remember Germany as a central crossroads between western Europe, the Baltic
side and the Alps. Reunification is the historical hook attached to that
central position.

7
00:01:15,985 --> 00:01:20,455
Let's bring Switzerland and its neighbours into a closer view.

8
00:01:23,303 --> 00:01:34,309
Switzerland sits in the Alps, below Germany and between France, Italy and
Austria. Liechtenstein is tucked against its eastern side.

9
00:01:34,966 --> 00:01:45,926
Switzerland has four national languages: German, French, Italian and Romansh.
Its mountain setting holds several language communities within one country.

10
00:01:46,579 --> 00:01:58,177
Picture a multilingual Alpine crossroads. France is west, Italy south, Germany
north; keep Switzerland inland, rather than looking for a coast.

11
00:01:59,227 --> 00:02:05,276
Let's locate Liechtenstein on a labelled supplemental map, then zoom in to see
its outline.

12
00:02:12,619 --> 00:02:22,174
Liechtenstein is the small Alpine country between Switzerland and Austria. The
Rhine runs along much of its western boundary beside Switzerland.

13
00:02:22,837 --> 00:02:31,626
It is doubly landlocked: both its neighbours are landlocked too. You must
cross at least two national borders to reach an open sea.

14
00:02:32,279 --> 00:02:45,375
Remember the tiny slot between Switzerland and Austria: landlocked neighbours
around a landlocked country. This labelled supplemental map shows its actual
outline.

15
00:02:46,161 --> 00:02:50,086
Let's bring Austria and its neighbours into a closer view.

16
00:02:53,398 --> 00:03:04,996
Austria stretches east from Switzerland and Liechtenstein, below Germany and
Czechia. Italy and Slovenia lie to the south, with Hungary toward its eastern
end.

17
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:17,514
Vienna became a major European centre of music, associated with composers
including Mozart and Beethoven. The city of music gives Austria a memorable
cultural anchor.

18
00:03:18,172 --> 00:03:30,711
Keep Austria as the long Alpine country east of Switzerland. Its eastern end
points toward Vienna and the Danube, leading us toward the countries farther
east.

19
00:03:31,396 --> 00:03:36,249
Let's bring Poland and its neighbours into a closer view.

20
00:03:39,093 --> 00:03:51,458
Poland is the broad country east of Germany and north of Czechia and Slovakia.
Its northern edge reaches the Baltic; Belarus and Ukraine lie to the east.

21
00:03:52,123 --> 00:04:06,159
Poland regained independence in 1918 after 123 years of partition. Its people
and culture continued even while an independent Polish state was absent from
the map.

22
00:04:06,817 --> 00:04:19,228
Picture Poland above the Czechia-Slovakia pair, with a Baltic opening at the
top. The lost-and-restored state is a historical hook, not a description of
today’s borders.

23
00:04:22,617 --> 00:04:31,487
Czechia is the inland country below Poland, east of Germany and north of
Austria. Slovakia lies immediately to its east.

24
00:04:32,150 --> 00:04:43,156
Charles University in Prague was founded in 1348. Picture an old university
city inside this landlocked country, rather than confusing it with a coastal
state.

25
00:04:43,817 --> 00:04:58,217
Remember the west-east pair: Czechia, then Slovakia. Czechia touches Germany;
Slovakia stretches onward toward Ukraine. Slovenia is a different country
farther south.

26
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:10,741
Slovakia is the narrow inland country east of Czechia, below Poland and above
Hungary. Its eastern tip reaches Ukraine.

27
00:05:11,404 --> 00:05:27,310
Bratislava, its capital, was a coronation city for the Hungarian kingdom
between 1563 and 1830. Present-day borders and historical kingdoms do not
always line up.

28
00:05:27,972 --> 00:05:40,847
Keep Slovakia above Hungary and east of Czechia. To separate the similar
names, remember Slovakia beside Czechia; Slovenia will appear beside the
Adriatic countries later.

29
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:55,976
Hungary lies below Slovakia and east of Austria, in the inland basin crossed
by the Danube. Romania is to the east, with Serbia and Croatia toward the
south.

30
00:05:56,637 --> 00:06:07,643
Erno Rubik invented his famous rotating cube in Budapest in 1974. The puzzle
gives Hungary a colourful memory hook in the middle of this inland group.

31
00:06:08,305 --> 00:06:20,414
Picture the Rubik’s Cube below Slovakia and beside Austria. Keep Hungary away
from the coast: it is an inland country with the Danube running through it.

32
00:06:26,610 --> 00:06:40,043
Germany anchors the north; Switzerland and Austria follow the Alps, with tiny
Liechtenstein between them. Remember reunification, four languages, the doubly
landlocked country, and Vienna’s music.

33
00:06:40,694 --> 00:06:53,871
Farther east, Poland sits above Czechia and Slovakia; Hungary lies below
Slovakia. Keep Slovakia beside Czechia, not confused with Slovenia. Those
eight places complete our central group.
